 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Complexity:** Naive evaluation requires $O(K H_{SR} W_{SR})$ operations. Optimized via a CUDA rasterizer with dual culling (bounding box and quantum turning point) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Rmax culling accelerates rendering up to 65% without quality loss, proving Branes resolve complexity without dense overlap [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Rmax culling accelerates rendering without quality loss, proving Branes resolve complexity without dense overlap [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces the number of overlapping primitives needed to model high frequencies compared to standard Gaussian Splatting. Provides superior speed-quality trade-off than prior GS methods in ASR tasks [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Predicting excessive modes (e.g., $N=M=7$) in a single pass can hamper generalization and cause smooth-region artifacts if not properly regularized [S1, unofficial]. Shifting to YCbCr color space slightly degrades performance compared to RGB due to asymmetric distributions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Arbitrary-Scale Super-Resolution tasks requiring high-frequency detail reconstruction with limited primitive counts.
-*   **When not to use:** Standard fixed-resolution rendering where standard Gaussians are sufficient, or when computational resources are extremely limited and culling overhead is prohibitive.
